From 52642393462ef32524fc6478350f97e2c6abb2c5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 16:36:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): walk dsv4_hc_post one stream row at a time
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The CPU kernel of DSV4_HC_POST splits a flat dst index into (i0, idst,
it) for every element. The build at -O3 runs 3 to 4 64-bit idiv per
element in the hot loop and no vector code. The Zen 2 divider is not
pipelined, so the op takes about 5.5 ms per call in a 512-token qwen4exp
ubatch (96 calls, 4.7% of pp512), where its 47 MB of traffic needs about
0.5 ms. qwen4exp builds this fused op since #28901, and the fusion
carries the tg gain, so only the kernel changes.

Each thread keeps its flat element range [ir0, ir1), so every thread
still works at n_tokens = 1. The new path walks that range one stream
row segment at a time, with one divide per segment, and the inner loops
run with unit stride over n_embd. It runs when x, residual and dst have
contiguous rows and use_ref is off. The scalar loop stays as the
fallback and as the reference for test-backend-ops.

The result is bit-exact with the scalar loop under GCC -O3
-march=native (znver2) and its default -ffp-contract=fast:

- Identity (comb == NULL, qwen4exp): one pass writes x*post and
  ggml_vec_acc_f32 adds the residual. A single loop would contract into
  an FMA. The base object shows vmulss then vaddss for the scalar loop.
- Comb (DeepSeek V4): the same per-element chain over the streams in
  the same order. The base object shows the scalar chain as vmulss and
  vaddss, because GCC avoids FMA chains on znver2. The new chain has
  the same shape and gets the same choice.

The row-segment pattern follows the dsv4_hc_pre change. Add test cases
whose thread ranges start mid-row (n_embd 1000, n_tokens 3), an uneven
token count (33), and the decode and prefill shapes of qwen4exp.

